Recoil [
    Title: "Brom audio engine descriptor"
    Description: {
        The contract a plug-in's DSP satisfies so that one shared host shell can
        drive it. This is the Recoil form of Brom's AudioEngine/SynthEngine
        traits: Recoil has no traits, but a trait object is a table of function
        pointers, and a c-struct! of fn-ptr! fields is exactly that.

        Why it exists: kapralova/clap/clap_entry.rcl (1670 lines) and
        loos/clap/clap_entry.rcl (1762) differ by 250 lines once the plug-in
        name is normalised away. The other ~85% -- event dispatch, audio and
        note ports, parameter plumbing, state, GUI lifecycle -- is host glue
        that has nothing to do with either synth. A third copy for Mach would
        make it ~5,100 lines of duplicated boilerplate.

        The engine state itself is opaque. Every entry point takes a `core`
        c-pointer! that the shell only ever passes back, so the shell needs no
        knowledge of the plug-in's voice structs, and the plug-in needs none of
        CLAP.

        Parameters are described, not hardcoded: `param-info-fn` fills a
        param-info! for an index, and the range carries a brom/plugin/values
        taper, so a host sees the same curve the editor does.
    }
    Type: module
    Module: brom/plugin/engine
    Ffi: [c-string]
    Imports: [
        brom/plugin/values [param-range! param-denormalize param-normalize]
    ]
    Exports: [
        engine! param-info! brom-voice-state!
        BROM-MAX-VOICES BROM-MAX-PARAMS BROM-MAX-STATE-BYTES
        engine-param-info engine-param-count brom-select-voice
        engine-set-param-normalized engine-get-param-normalized
    ]
]

; Ceilings on the fixed-size bookkeeping the shared CLAP shell keeps per
; instance. They exist because a c-struct! field's c-array! length must be a
; compile-time constant, and the shell is compiled once for every plug-in --
; it cannot size itself from a descriptor it only sees at run time.
;
; The cost of headroom is a few KB per plug-in instance, so these are set well
; above what any current plug-in needs (Kapralova 32 voices / 12 params, Loos
; 4 / 12) rather than tuned. An engine declaring more is rejected at create
; time instead of overrunning.
BROM-MAX-VOICES: 64
BROM-MAX-PARAMS: 256
; 16 KB rather than 4: a plug-in whose state is preset text (see
; brom/plugin/preset) spends roughly 45 bytes per parameter it has moved away
; from its default, so Mach's 124 parameters need about 5 KB in the worst case
; and 4096 would truncate a fully-dialled patch. The cost is per plug-in
; INSTANCE in the shell's fixed buffer, which is the trade: a few kilobytes
; against a session that silently fails to save.
BROM-MAX-STATE-BYTES: 16384

; What Brom needs to know about one voice to rank it for stealing. Four facts,
; none of them instrument-specific -- which is the point: voice allocation is
; the same problem for an electric piano, a sawtooth synth and an additive
; engine, so it is solved once here instead of in each plug-in's DSP core.
;
; `active` and `gate` are deliberately different questions. `gate` is "the
; player is still holding this key"; `active` is "this voice is still making
; sound", which stays true through the release tail. Ranking on the wrong one
; steals a voice that is still audible and clicks.
brom-voice-state!: make struct! [
    active: logic!
    gate: logic!
    energy: f64!
    age: i64!
]

; One parameter's description, filled by the plug-in on demand rather than held
; in a table, so the shell never owns plug-in-shaped storage.
;
; `name` and `module` are borrowed static strings; the shell copies them into
; whatever fixed buffer the host ABI wants. `module` is the group a host shows
; the parameter under ("Tine", "Pickup", ...); empty means ungrouped.
;
; `range` carries the taper, so `param-denormalize` here and the editor's knob
; agree by construction rather than by two copies of the same constants.
param-info!: make struct! [
    id: u32!
    name: c-string!
    module: c-string!
    default-value: f64!
    range: param-range!
]

; The descriptor a plug-in fills in once, statically.
;
; Field names all carry an `-fn` suffix on purpose: function-pointer struct
; fields resolve globally by field name in the parser, so a bare `reset` or
; `process` here would collide with any same-named non-callable field elsewhere
; in the program and turn every `x/reset` into an attempted call.
;
; Every entry point takes `core` -- the opaque pointer `create-fn` returned --
; except `param-info-fn`, which describes the plug-in as a class rather than an
; instance and so must be callable before any instance exists (a host asks for
; parameter metadata during scanning).
engine!: make c-struct! [
    ; --- identity, read by the shell when it answers the host's scan ---
    id: [c-string!]
    name: [c-string!]
    vendor: [c-string!]
    version: [c-string!]
    description: [c-string!]

    param-count: [i32!]
    ; Voice slots the shell keeps CLAP-side bookkeeping for. It must not exceed
    ; BROM-MAX-VOICES, which sizes that bookkeeping.
    max-voices: [i32!]
    ; Bytes the engine's state blob occupies -- fixed, so the shell can stream it
    ; without a length prefix and existing saved patches keep loading.
    state-size: [i32!]
    ; Frames the buffer returned by output-fn can hold; the shell renders in
    ; chunks no larger than this.
    output-capacity: [i32!]
    editor-width: [i32!]
    editor-height: [i32!]
    ; Whether this plug-in has an editor at all. False makes the shell withhold
    ; the clap.gui extension entirely, so a host shows its own generic parameter
    ; UI instead of asking for a window nothing can draw. Defaults false because
    ; a c-struct! field zero-initializes; every descriptor that wants an editor
    ; sets it explicitly.
    has-editor: [logic!]

    ; --- lifecycle ---
    create-fn: [fn-ptr! [return: [c-pointer!]]]
    destroy-fn: [fn-ptr! [:core [c-pointer!] return: [none!]]]
    activate-fn: [fn-ptr! [
        :core [c-pointer!]
        sample-rate [f64!]
        max-frames [i32!]
        return: [logic!]
    ]]
    reset-fn: [fn-ptr! [:core [c-pointer!] return: [none!]]]

    ; --- voices ---
    ; Reports one voice's state so brom-select-voice can rank it. An engine
    ; supplies facts about its voices and Brom owns the stealing policy, rather
    ; than every plug-in writing its own ranking loop.
    voice-state-fn: [fn-ptr! [
        :core [c-pointer!]
        slot [i32!]
        :out [c-pointer!]
        return: [logic!]
    ]]

    ; Set only by an engine that wants a policy brom-select-voice cannot
    ; express; when false, voice-slot-fn is never called and may be left null.
    custom-voice-slot: [logic!]
    voice-slot-fn: [fn-ptr! [
        :core [c-pointer!]
        note-id [i32!]
        key [i32!]
        return: [i32!]
    ]]

    ; --- audio ---
    ; Renders `frames` into the engine's own output buffer; the shell then copies
    ; that buffer into the host's ports. This is the hot path and the boundary
    ; where branch-free inner loops start mattering.
    process-fn: [fn-ptr! [:core [c-pointer!] frames [i32!] return: [none!]]]
    ; Address of that buffer. Stable across a block, so the shell reads it once.
    output-fn: [fn-ptr! [:core [c-pointer!] return: [c-pointer!]]]

    ; `key` is the MIDI key the host sent; an engine that wants frequency
    ; converts it itself, so the shell never carries tuning policy.
    note-on-fn: [fn-ptr! [
        :core [c-pointer!]
        slot [i32!]
        key [i32!]
        velocity [f64!]
        return: [none!]
    ]]
    note-off-fn: [fn-ptr! [:core [c-pointer!] slot [i32!] return: [none!]]]

    ; --- parameters ---
    ; Fills `out` (a param-info!) for `index` in 0 .. param-count-1; false when
    ; the index is out of range.
    param-info-fn: [fn-ptr! [
        index [i32!]
        :out [c-pointer!]
        return: [logic!]
    ]]
    ; Values crossing these two are in the plug-in's own engineering units, not
    ; normalized -- the taper conversion happens in the shell, where the
    ; param-info! is already to hand.
    get-param-fn: [fn-ptr! [:core [c-pointer!] id [u32!] return: [f64!]]]
    set-param-fn: [fn-ptr! [
        :core [c-pointer!]
        id [u32!]
        value [f64!]
        return: [logic!]
    ]]

    ; --- state ---
    ; Superseded by the state-subsystem/state-version/state-save-ascii/
    ; has-state-context identity fields below, which brom/plugin/state/
    ; dispatch.rcl's brom-state-load/-save read to route every plug-in's
    ; state through one shared, format-aware mechanism. No CLAP or VST3
    ; shell calls save-state-fn/load-state-fn any more; Mach and Loos still
    ; wire them to their own hand-rolled functions, kept only until a future
    ; migration deletes them along with the functions themselves.
    save-state-fn: [fn-ptr! [
        :core [c-pointer!]
        out [c-pointer!]
        capacity [i32!]
        return: [logic!]
    ]]
    load-state-fn: [fn-ptr! [
        :core [c-pointer!]
        bytes [c-pointer!]
        length [i32!]
        return: [logic!]
    ]]

    ; --- optional modes ------------------------------------------------------
    ; Three shapes the first two plug-ins did not need and Mach does. Each is a
    ; flag plus the entry point it enables; every flag defaults to false, so an
    ; engine that ignores this block behaves exactly as before and may leave the
    ; matching fn-ptr! null. Added at the end of the struct on purpose -- a
    ; descriptor is plain data and existing ones must keep their field offsets.

    ; The engine allocates its own voices. When set, the shell does no slot
    ; bookkeeping at all: find-voice-slot and brom-select-voice are skipped,
    ; voice-state-fn and note-on-fn/note-off-fn are never called, and every note
    ; event goes to note-event-fn as the host sent it. This is a different
    ; question from custom-voice-slot, which only replaces the *ranking* while
    ; the shell still owns the slots.
    core-owned-voices: [logic!]
    ; `on?` distinguishes note-on from note-off/note-end; `velocity` is
    ; meaningless on a note-off and engines should ignore it there.
    note-event-fn: [fn-ptr! [
        :core [c-pointer!]
        on? [logic!]
        note-id [i32!]
        channel [i32!]
        key [i32!]
        velocity [f64!]
        return: [none!]
    ]]

    ; The engine renders two channels. output-fn keeps returning the left
    ; buffer and output-right-fn returns the right; process-fn fills both. When
    ; false the shell duplicates output-fn's mono buffer to every output
    ; channel, which is what Kapralova and Loos rely on.
    stereo-output: [logic!]
    output-right-fn: [fn-ptr! [:core [c-pointer!] return: [c-pointer!]]]

    ; The engine renders and parses its own parameter text. Brom needs no flag
    ; for *units* -- a value crossing get/set-param-fn means whatever the
    ; descriptor's param-range! says, so a plug-in reporting normalized 0..1
    ; already works by declaring lo 0.0 / hi 1.0. What the shell cannot do is
    ; display "-6.00 dB" for 0.635938, or read it back. Both directions are
    ; here because supporting only one leaves a host's typed entry silently
    ; writing a wrong value.
    custom-param-text: [logic!]
    param-text-fn: [fn-ptr! [
        :core [c-pointer!]
        id [u32!]
        value [f64!]
        out [c-pointer!]
        capacity [u32!]
        return: [logic!]
    ]]
    param-parse-fn: [fn-ptr! [
        :core [c-pointer!]
        id [u32!]
        text [c-string!]
        :out [c-pointer!]
        return: [logic!]
    ]]

    ; --- factory presets -----------------------------------------------------
    ; A plug-in's built-in patches, as preset text (see brom/plugin/preset).
    ; The text rather than a struct of values, because the preset format is
    ; already the plug-in-independent way to say "these parameters take these
    ; values" -- a bank is a list of those, and needs no second representation.
    ;
    ; Appended at the end so descriptors written before this keep their field
    ; offsets. A plug-in with no bank leaves factory-count at zero and the
    ; fn-ptr! null, and every preset entry point reports an empty bank.
    factory-count: [i32!]
    factory-preset-fn: [fn-ptr! [
        index [i32!]
        return: [string!]
    ]]

    ; --- state identity -------------------------------------------------------
    ; Pure data: which subsystem a binary state blob belongs to, that
    ; subsystem's own schema-shape version, and whether its plain save is
    ; ASCII preset text or the binary codec. codec.rcl and dispatch.rcl read
    ; these; no plug-in writes save/load code against them directly.
    ;
    ; Appended at the end, like factory-count above, so existing descriptors
    ; keep their field offsets. Every field defaults false/zero, so a
    ; descriptor that ignores this block gets state-subsystem 0 and
    ; state-save-ascii false -- a plug-in migrating onto the shared state
    ; mechanism must set these explicitly.
    state-subsystem: [u32!]
    state-version: [u32!]
    state-save-ascii: [logic!]
    ; Whether this plug-in advertises CLAP's clap.state-context extension.
    ; False withholds the extension entirely, the same way has-editor
    ; withholds clap.gui.
    has-state-context: [logic!]
]

brom-select-voice: func [
    "Pick the voice slot a new note should sound on, by Brom's shared stealing policy."
    :engine [engine!] "Borrowed engine descriptor."
    :core [c-pointer!] "Borrowed opaque engine instance."
    return: [i32!] "Slot in 0 .. max-voices-1, or -1 when the engine declares no voices."
] [
    count: make i32! engine/max-voices
    if count <= 0 [return -1]

    vs: make [#mutable brom-voice-state!] make brom-voice-state! [
        active: false gate: false energy: 0.0 age: 0
    ]

    ; Tier 1 -- a silent voice. Free to take, so nothing else is considered.
    repeat index count [
        if engine/voice-state-fn :core index (addr-of vs) [
            if not vs/active [return index]
        ]
    ]

    ; Tier 2 -- the quietest voice whose key the player has already lifted,
    ; oldest first on a tie. Cutting a voice that is fading out is far less
    ; audible than cutting one that is still being held.
    selected: make [#mutable i32!] -1
    selected-energy: make [#mutable f64!] 0.0
    selected-age: make [#mutable i64!] 0
    repeat index count [
        if engine/voice-state-fn :core index (addr-of vs) [
            if not vs/gate [
                if any [
                    selected < 0
                    vs/energy < selected-energy
                    all [vs/energy = selected-energy vs/age < selected-age]
                ] [
                    selected: index
                    selected-energy: vs/energy
                    selected-age: vs/age
                ]
            ]
        ]
    ]
    if selected >= 0 [return selected]

    ; Tier 3 -- every key is still down, so there is no released voice to take
    ; and something audible has to give: steal the oldest.
    selected: 0
    if engine/voice-state-fn :core 0 (addr-of vs) [selected-age: vs/age]
    repeat index count [
        if engine/voice-state-fn :core index (addr-of vs) [
            if vs/age < selected-age [
                selected: index
                selected-age: vs/age
            ]
        ]
    ]
    return selected
]

engine-param-count: func [
    "Number of parameters the engine exposes."
    :engine [engine!] "Borrowed engine descriptor."
    return: [i32!] "Parameter count; zero when the descriptor is empty."
] [
    return engine/param-count
]

engine-param-info: func [
    "Fill `out` with the description of the parameter at `index`."
    :engine [engine!] "Borrowed engine descriptor."
    index [i32!] "Parameter index in 0 .. param-count-1."
    :out [c-pointer!] "Borrowed address of a caller-owned param-info! to fill."
    return: [logic!] "False when the index is out of range or unhandled."
] [
    if index < 0 [return false]
    if index >= engine/param-count [return false]
    if none? out [return false]
    return engine/param-info-fn index :out
]

engine-set-param-normalized: func [
    "Set a parameter from a normalized 0.0-1.0 value, applying the parameter's own taper. This is the entry point a host or an editor knob feeds, both of which speak normalized."
    :engine [engine!] "Borrowed engine descriptor."
    :core [c-pointer!] "Borrowed opaque engine instance."
    index [i32!] "Parameter index in 0 .. param-count-1."
    normalized [f64!] "Position in 0.0-1.0."
    return: [logic!] "False when the index is out of range."
] [
    info: make [#mutable param-info!] make param-info! [
        id: 0 name: (as c-string! "") module: (as c-string! "") default-value: 0.0
        range: make param-range! [
            lo: 0.0 hi: 1.0 skew: 1.0 symmetric: 0 steps: 0 kind: 0
        ]
    ]
    if not engine-param-info :engine index (addr-of info) [return false]
    return engine/set-param-fn :core info/id (param-denormalize info/range normalized)
]

engine-get-param-normalized: func [
    "Read a parameter back as a normalized 0.0-1.0 value, inverting the parameter's taper."
    :engine [engine!] "Borrowed engine descriptor."
    :core [c-pointer!] "Borrowed opaque engine instance."
    index [i32!] "Parameter index in 0 .. param-count-1."
    return: [f64!] "Position in 0.0-1.0; 0.0 when the index is out of range."
] [
    info: make [#mutable param-info!] make param-info! [
        id: 0 name: (as c-string! "") module: (as c-string! "") default-value: 0.0
        range: make param-range! [
            lo: 0.0 hi: 1.0 skew: 1.0 symmetric: 0 steps: 0 kind: 0
        ]
    ]
    if not engine-param-info :engine index (addr-of info) [return 0.0]
    return param-normalize info/range (engine/get-param-fn :core info/id)
]
